Why: (1) the VPU sleep/wake handshake is broken on this silicon - wake after
sleep never completes - so the suspend/resume sleep and wake calls are skipped
(the vendor never uses them either). (2) Upstream allocates codec DMA from the
device's default (global CMA) path; this routes all codec DMA (DPB, WTL/display
buffers, vb_task) to the SoC's dedicated mmz reserved-memory region via
of_reserved_mem, keeping the codec working set out of general CMA. The
attachment is released through a devres action: of_reserved_mem_device_init has
no devres form, so without one it survives every probe-failure return below it
and the remove path, and a rebind then inherits stale dma_mem state.

(3) The probe no longer writes 0x0a106018. That word is the display controller's
CRG (artosyn_vo CRG_DC), which the display bring-up read-modify-writes unlocked,
so two drivers raced on one register. It was set here as a belt-and-braces venc
clock gate, but the venc gate is 0x0a104018 BIT(12), outside this page and
already set by the time the codec probes.

(4) inject_lost_enc_irq. Drops N encoder completions to reproduce the lost-interrupt
failure on demand, which is the case the job timeout in the encoder exists to cover
and the only one no test could previously reach. Default 0, which changes nothing.

(5) /sys/kernel/debug/wave5. state reports the registers that separate a healthy
VPU from a poisoned one - a VCPU program counter frozen at one address with
BUSY_STATUS still set is the wedge signature - plus the live instances and their
job-timeout and firmware-fault counters. recover performs the global block reset
and firmware re-download from 0240. Both take a pm_runtime reference first,
because runtime suspend gates the codec clocks and the registers read back
garbage without it.

